before:
Here is an object you can create and inspect.
```javascript
const character = {
  name: "Rowan",
  health: 12,
  equipment: ["compass", "rope"]
};
console.log(character.name);
console.log(character.health);
console.log(character.equipment[0]);
```
The outputs are Rowan, 12, and compass. Quotes make the name and equipment text. The health is numeric. The final line reads the equipment array, then its first entry at index 0.

This second example changes only the health.
```javascript
const character = {
  name: "Rowan",
  health: 12,
  equipment: ["compass", "rope"]
};
character.health = 9;
console.log(character.name, character.health);
console.log(character.equipment[0]);
```
It prints `Rowan 9`, then `compass`. The property assignment replaces 12 with 9. It does not add 9 to 12, and it does not remove or rename the equipment property.

after:
Create and read one character:
```javascript
const character = {
  name: "Rowan",
  health: 12,
  equipment: ["compass", "rope"]
};
console.log(character.name);
console.log(character.health);
console.log(character.equipment[0]);
```
Output:
```text
Rowan
12
compass
```
The equipment is an array. Position 0 gives its first item.

Change only the health:
```javascript
const character = {
  name: "Rowan",
  health: 12,
  equipment: ["compass", "rope"]
};
character.health = 9;
console.log(character.name, character.health);
console.log(character.equipment[0]);
```
Output:
```text
Rowan 9
compass
```
The assignment replaces 12 with 9. The other properties stay as they were.
